    "body": "### Summary\n\nWhen `/v1/querybatch` answers `200` with fewer `results` than the queries it was sent, `uv audit` pairs queries with results by position and silently drops the unmatched queries. The packages whose results are missing are never checked, but the command prints `Found no known vulnerabilities …` and exits `0`. A truncated or empty `200` body — from an intermediary proxy, a captive portal that happens to return JSON, or a service-side bug — therefore turns into a green audit.\n\n### Reproduction (uv 0.12.21)\n\n`pyproject.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[project]\nname = \"repro\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.12\"\ndependencies = [\"python-multipart==0.0.6\"]\n```\n\n`fake_osv.py`, an OSV stand-in that answers `200` with `N` fewer results than queries:\n\n```python\nimport json, sys\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\n\nclass H(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def log_message(self, *a): pass\n    def do_POST(self):\n        n = len(json.loads(self.rfile.read(int(self.headers[\"Content-Length\"])))[\"queries\"])\n        body = json.dumps({\"results\": [{}] * max(n - int(sys.argv[2]), 0)}).encode()\n        self.send_response(200); self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body))); self.end_headers(); self.wfile.write(body)\n\nHTTPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", int(sys.argv[1])), H).serve_forever()\n```\n\n```console\n$ uv lock\n$ uv audit --frozen --preview-features audit-command                     # real OSV\n  ...\n  Advisory information: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-40347\n$ echo $?\n1\n\n$ python3 fake_osv.py 18777 1 &                                          # one result short\n$ uv audit --frozen --preview-features audit-command --service-url http://127.0.0.1:18777\nFound no known vulnerabilities and no adverse project statuses in 1 package\n$ echo $?\n0\n\n$ python3 fake_osv.py 18778 999 &                                        # {\"results\": []}\n$ uv audit --frozen --preview-features audit-command --service-url http://127.0.0.1:18778\nFound no known vulnerabilities and no adverse project statuses in 1 package\n$ echo $?\n0\n```\n\nAlso reproduced on 0.12.9 and 0.12.19.\n\n### Expected\n\nA response whose `results` length differs from the number of queries in the batch is an error (exit `2`, like other service failures), not a clean result.\n\n### Where\n\n`crates/uv-audit/src/service/osv.rs`, `query_identifiers`, on `main`:\n\n```rust\npending_batch.iter().zip(batch_response.results.iter())\n```\n\n`zip` stops at the shorter side. A length check before it (`batch_response.results.len() == pending_batch.len()`, otherwise an error) would close this. OSV's API documents one result per query, in order.\n\n### Why it matters\n\n`uv audit` is typically used as a CI gate or a scheduled check, where a false \"clean\" is the one failure nobody looks at. We found this while hardening a nightly audit job and currently guard against it with a known-vulnerable canary lock that must come back vulnerable before the real locks are trusted.\n",
