    "pull_request": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/astral-sh/uv/pulls/22057",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/astral-sh/uv/pull/22057",
      "diff_url": "https://github.com/astral-sh/uv/pull/22057.diff",
      "patch_url": "https://github.com/astral-sh/uv/pull/22057.patch",
      "merged_at": null
    },
    "body": "## Summary\n\nConcurrent replacement of files in one directory can make ext4 start delayed-allocation writeback while rename holds the directory lock. Move that writeback outside the replacing rename in `uv_fs::write_atomic_sync`, on top of the batched HTTP cache writes in #22056.\n\nOn Linux, write through the temporary file's original descriptor and attempt `persist_noclobber`, which uses `RENAME_NOREPLACE` where supported. New files are published without a separate destination lookup and retain delayed allocation. If publication fails, reopen the temporary file with truncation, rewrite the payload, and close that writer before the regular replacing rename. This makes ext4's `auto_da_alloc` start writeback on close, outside the directory lock.\n\nThe fallback also handles filesystems that reject no-clobber persistence. Existing destinations, including dangling symlinks, are still atomically replaced. Other platforms continue to write through the original descriptor and use their normal persistence path. Replacements write the payload twice; new-file writes avoid both the extra write and the destination metadata lookup.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n- Added integration tests for creation, replacement, empty contents, replacement of dangling and existing symlinks, and temporary-file cleanup after a failed replacement. All three tests passed on Linux and macOS.\n- All six `uv-client` cache-client integration tests passed on macOS.\n- `cargo clippy --locked -p uv-fs --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` passed on Linux and macOS, as did the equivalent Windows check with `cargo xwin clippy`.\n- Rust formatting and diff checks passed.\n- Ran the unchanged `atomic_write` and `cached_client` benchmarks twice on a native Docker ext4 volume (Linux 7.0.12, aarch64, Rust 1.98.1, 8 CPUs), reversing version order for the second pass. Each batch processes 500 files with 256 KiB payloads at concurrency 50; setup and cleanup are outside the timer. The disposable benchmark build retained only the dependencies used by these two benches. Ranges below are the Criterion point estimates across the two runs:\n\n| Workload                                        | Batched writes alone (#22056) | With this fix |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------: | ------------: |\n| Replace files in one directory, blocking        |                534.7–603.9 ms |  19.8–21.1 ms |\n| Replace files in separate directories, blocking |                  15.5–18.1 ms |  17.0–19.1 ms |\n| Create files in one directory, blocking         |                  21.9–25.3 ms |  19.0–19.8 ms |\n| HTTP cache revalidation                         |                538.7–544.2 ms |  28.8–29.9 ms |\n\nThese runs reproduce the ext4 replacement slowdown but do not reproduce the CI new-file regression. CI measurements are still needed to establish the effect on that runner.\n",
